            ("p", "A mat has a hard surface under a thin layer of synthetic fibre. Hit two inches behind the ball and the club skids off that surface into the back of the ball, producing a shot that looks respectable. Do the same on turf and the club digs, the ball goes nowhere, and you get an honest report on your low point control."),
            ("p", "That honesty is the whole point. Golfers who practise exclusively on mats often arrive at the first tee with a swing that has been quietly rewarded for a fault, then wonder why the same swing produces heavy shots on the course. Turf practice removes the disguise, which is uncomfortable for a session or two and extremely valuable after that."),
